          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which conditions covered here have a three-character code that takes no further character?",
          "answer": "Based on the coding guidance I retrieved, only one condition in the material is a three-character code with nothing after it:\n\n- **Essential (primary) hypertension, I10.** The hypertension document says I10 \"is unusual in being a complete code at three characters.\" Most other categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before they are complete. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\nThe other three-character entries I found, J44 (COPD) and E11 (type 2 diabetes), are categories that need further characters. The COPD and diabetes documents say their fourth or later characters add detail, such as whether a complication is present. (Sources: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44* and *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*.)\n\nThis is a coding question about the classification system, not a statement about any person's health. The retrieved material did not cover every ICD-10-CM category, so I cannot say whether other three-character codes also stand alone.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
